    "narrative": "For LawDog on August 29, the lineup is optimized to exploit soft matchups, specifically against Ryan Johnson. **Bryce Harper** headlines our attack with a dominant 77.13 score, bolstered by a weak 5.16 xERA opposing starter and a favorable park factor. Close behind, **Lawrence Butler** (73.46) and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (71.93) are our pillars of consistency. **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from a platoon advantage and a projected spot in the heart of the order, while **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains a plug-and-play elite option despite facing a lefty, as his high base production consistently overcomes marginal platoon shifts. We are also looking for a meaningful contribution from **Jung Hoo Lee**, whose 65.64 score is elevated by an favorable wind condition and a soft matchup against Merrill Kelly.\n\nThe most difficult decision at the margins was excluding **Dylan Beavers**, who sat at 53.09. While he offered decent upside against Jack Perkins, he was ultimately bypassed because his projected efficiency failed to crack the top-tier threshold held by the likes of **Luis Arraez** (66.77) and **Justin Crawford** (64.16). While **Justin Crawford** faces a penalty for a projected #9 batting spot, his elite speed profile in a 5x5 Roto format makes him a tactical necessity over the bench options. Note that several players, including **Zack Gelof**, **Lawrence Butler**, **Bryce Harper**, **Justin Crawford**, and **Luis Arraez**, are currently marked as \"Lineup Pending.\" Should these players bat lower than the assumed order, their efficiency scores\u2014particularly those of **Zack Gelof** and **Justin Crawford**\u2014will require immediate re-evaluation. **Gleyber Torres** carries the highest risk today, as his score of 55.51 is suppressed by the difficult 3.40 xERA presented by Blake Snell, making him a player to watch closely for any late-breaking lineup changes.",
